    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10d-catfishes-from-africa/synodontis_batensoda_en/",
      "html": "<p>Quite a number of Synodontis species tend to swim upside down. The best known is the upside-down cat, Synodontis nigriventris from the Congo. Synodontis batensoda shows a similar behaviour.</p><p>It seems to be quite unimportant for the fish which position it actually swims in. However, the very dark coloration of the belly shows that the upside-down position is used very often. Fish species with a \"normal\" swimming behaviour have light bellies, so they do not contrast much with the sky. Many predatory fish hunt from below, so this coloration is necessary.</p><p>The species is quite social and should never be kept alone. S. batensoda will eat small fish, but is usually very peaceful towards other, larger species.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10d-catfishes-from-africa/synodontis_batensoda_en/",
      "html": "<p>Formerly S. batensoda was placed in the monotypic genus Brachysynodontis.</p>"
    }
  ],
